## What it does

An Item aggregate owns the observations and decisions for one canonical HTTP or HTTPS URL.
It commits events and state together in one [Durable Object](/lore/workers-answer-requests-durable-objects-own-named-state).
Operators send `RecordSighting`, `Keep`, and `Drop` through one authenticated Api.
Recording a decision never publishes content.

`CatalogIndex` copies committed events into D1 through the `CatalogStore` [port](/lore/hexagonal-architecture).
The `Rising` and `RepeatAuthors` Queries read that derived index.
The public site receives no catalog data.

## The standard

- The Item event log is authoritative. D1 and `ItemHistory` are rebuildable copies.
- The first sighting from each source on each UTC day wins. A retry with another command id adds no observation.
- Item kinds are repo, post, video, and news. An observed item's kind cannot change.
- Keep and Drop require a sighting. An opposite decision can follow; repeating the current decision records nothing.
- Unknown metrics stay absent. A missing measurement never becomes zero.
- Every command, history read, and Query requires the operator token. Missing or empty configuration denies access.
- Domain's Api defines the [contract](/systems/capabilities). Its native projections expose the same schemas and authorization.

`CatalogIndex` keys each row by `stream:seq`.
A repeated delivery leaves the same row in place.
D1 batches commit together; the Domain checkpoint advances separately after the write succeeds.
A retry between these commits adds no duplicate rows.

### Trend queries

Both Queries use observation time in an explicit UTC window: `from` is inclusive and `until` is exclusive.
The window must span more than zero and at most 30 days.
Pages default to 50 entries, with a maximum of 200 and an opaque continuation cursor.
A cursor belongs to its Query and filters.

`Rising` compares the first and last known `stars` or `views` measurement within each item and source.
It requires two measurements and a positive gain.
It returns the strongest source once per item, ordered by gain and then canonical URL.

Each row includes `from`, `to`, absolute `gain`, `relativeGain`, measurement times, source, and the latest indexed decision.
Relative gain divides gain by `from`; a zero baseline returns null.
The decision follows the latest indexed Keep/Drop event, even outside the observation window.
Keep and Drop do not filter these results.

`RepeatAuthors` counts distinct canonical item URLs per exact author handle.
Repeated sightings of the same item do not increase that count.
The caller supplies `minimumItems` and up to 500 `excludedAuthors`.
Results sort by item count and then handle.

D1 uses indexed time-range reads.
The page limit bounds returned rows; aggregation still scans observations inside the selected window.
These initial bounds follow a local SQLite baseline.
Deployed D1 latency remains unproven.

### Known limits

For `RepeatAuthors`, automatic exclusion of admitted people waits for the Person slice.
The caller supplies exclusions until that source exists.
There is no Person aggregate, `Feature` command, or JSONL importer yet.

The D1 index can lag committed events.
Pass command receipts as `atLeast` to wait for their `CatalogIndex` checkpoints.
Receipts cover only their streams.
Results can change between pages as new observations arrive.

History state holds the latest observation and decision; `ItemHistory.list` pages through earlier events.
Operators pass an explicit `take` on each history list call.
Domain beta.87 supplies no default or maximum for `View.list`; omission reads the item's full history.
The Query page limits above do not change that native history behavior.

## How to check

Run `pnpm --filter @rat-stack/catalog test` for stories, declarations, and generated command histories.
`Domain.verify(Catalog)` must return no problems.

Run `pnpm --filter @rat-stack/catalog-server test` for both store implementations, replay, windows, authorization, and native HTTP smoke behavior.
SQLite exercises the D1 [adapter](/lore/hexagonal-architecture)'s SQL and migrations locally; it does not prove deployed D1 behavior.

Review `pnpm infra:plan` before deployment.
The owner supplies `CATALOG_OPERATOR_TOKEN` through deploy configuration; the [Worker](/lore/workers-answer-requests-durable-objects-own-named-state) binds a supplied value as a redacted secret.
No secret value ships in source.
Provisioning or rotating this configuration requires a deploy.

Run `pnpm --filter @rat-stack/catalog-server smoke <url>` with the token supplied securely through the environment.
The smoke uses typed `ItemHistory.get` calls: no token must return Unauthorized; the configured token must succeed or report a missing item.
It prints fixed results and sanitized failures, never the token.
A raw GET is neither an authentication check nor a health probe: an empty native [RPC](/skills/learn-rat-stack) request returns an empty NDJSON response.

Run `pnpm env:check`, then `pnpm turbo run check test build` for the full [fence](/lore/the-fence).
